window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["sql"]=[{"id":"sql-f0001","card":"q-sql-001-overview","sec":"sql-s001","q":"What does it mean that SQL is a declarative language, and why does that matter?","a":"<p>You describe the result you want, and the database engine's query optimizer decides how to get it (which indexes, which join algorithm, which order). You do not write loops or access paths. The benefit is that the same query can run faster after an index or statistics change without rewriting it. The cost is that you must understand how the optimizer reads your query when you tune it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0002","card":"q-sql-001-overview","sec":"sql-s001","q":"SQL is a standard, so why can the same query fail on a different database such as SQL Server, MySQL or PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>Every vendor implements the ANSI/ISO standard plus its own extensions: SQL Server uses T-SQL, Oracle uses PL/SQL, PostgreSQL uses PL/pgSQL. Row limiting is a common example: <code>TOP</code> in SQL Server, <code>LIMIT</code> in MySQL and PostgreSQL. String concatenation, date functions and temp-table syntax also differ. Prefer standard forms (<code>CAST</code>, <code>COALESCE</code>, <code>JOIN ... ON</code>) when portability matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0003","card":"q-sql-002-the-five-command-groups","sec":"sql-s002","q":"Name the five SQL command groups and give the main statements of each.","a":"<p>DDL defines structure (<code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code>). DML changes data (<code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>). DQL reads data (<code>SELECT</code>). DCL controls access (<code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code>; SQL Server also has <code>DENY</code>). TCL controls transactions (<code>COMMIT</code>, <code>ROLLBACK</code>, <code>SAVEPOINT</code>). Some texts count <code>SELECT</code> as part of DML.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0004","card":"q-sql-002-the-five-command-groups","sec":"sql-s002","q":"Compare <code>DELETE</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code> and <code>DROP TABLE</code>. Which one would you use to empty a table, and which one to remove it entirely?","a":"<p><code>DELETE</code> is DML. It removes rows one by one, accepts a <code>WHERE</code>, fires row triggers and is fully logged. <code>TRUNCATE</code> is DDL. It removes all rows at once with minimal logging, accepts no <code>WHERE</code> and resets identity counters. In SQL Server it can be rolled back inside a transaction. <code>DROP TABLE</code> removes the rows and the table definition itself. To empty a table use <code>TRUNCATE</code> (all rows) or <code>DELETE</code> (some rows). To remove the table use <code>DROP</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0005","card":"q-sql-002-the-five-command-groups","sec":"sql-s002","q":"Why is <code>TRUNCATE</code> classed as DDL even though it only removes data?","a":"<p>It works on the table as a whole (it deallocates data pages and resets the identity seed) instead of operating on individual rows, so it behaves like a structural command. That is also why it has no <code>WHERE</code> clause and does not fire <code>DELETE</code> triggers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0006","card":"q-sql-002-the-five-command-groups","sec":"sql-s002","q":"Why do <code>COMMIT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK</code> matter for DML but usually not for plain <code>SELECT</code>?","a":"<p>DML changes (<code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>) are part of a transaction, so you can make them permanent with <code>COMMIT</code> or undo them with <code>ROLLBACK</code>. A <code>SELECT</code> changes nothing, so there is nothing to undo. Whether DDL can also be rolled back depends on the database. In SQL Server and PostgreSQL DDL is transactional. In MySQL and Oracle most DDL commits implicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0007","card":"q-sql-002-the-five-command-groups","sec":"sql-s002","q":"A developer needs to read one table but must never change it. Which command group handles this and which statements do you use?","a":"<p>DCL. Grant only <code>SELECT</code> on that table to the user or role (<code>GRANT SELECT ON dbo.orders TO reporting_role</code>), and use <code>REVOKE</code> to remove rights later. This follows the least-privilege principle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0008","card":"q-sql-003-create-and-use-a-database","sec":"sql-s003","q":"In SQL Server, what do <code>CREATE DATABASE</code> and <code>USE</code> each do, and what goes wrong if you forget <code>USE</code>?","a":"<p><code>CREATE DATABASE</code> creates the database. <code>USE</code> switches the current connection to it. If you forget <code>USE</code>, later <code>CREATE TABLE</code> statements run in whatever database the session is already in, often <code>master</code>, and you pollute the wrong database. You can also qualify names (<code>mydb.dbo.tbl</code>) to avoid depending on context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0009","card":"q-sql-003-create-and-use-a-database","sec":"sql-s003","q":"Which part of the <code>USE</code> statement is not portable across databases?","a":"<p><code>USE dbname</code> works in SQL Server and MySQL. PostgreSQL has no <code>USE</code>; you connect to a specific database (<code>\\c dbname</code> in psql, or a new connection). Oracle works with schemas inside one database instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0010","card":"q-sql-004-create-a-table-with-constraints","sec":"sql-s004","q":"What does <code>identity(1,1)</code> mean on a column in SQL Server, and can you rely on it having no gaps?","a":"<p>The first number is the seed (first value) and the second is the increment, so ids are 1, 2, 3 and so on. Gaps are normal: a rolled-back insert or a deleted row still consumes a value. Use it for a surrogate key, not for a gapless business number. Other databases use <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> (MySQL), <code>SERIAL</code> or <code>IDENTITY</code> columns (PostgreSQL).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0011","card":"q-sql-004-create-a-table-with-constraints","sec":"sql-s004","q":"What does each of these constraints do: <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>, <code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>CHECK</code>, <code>DEFAULT</code>?","a":"<p><code>PRIMARY KEY</code> makes the column unique and not null, and identifies the row (one per table). <code>NOT NULL</code> rejects missing values. <code>UNIQUE</code> rejects duplicates (a table can have several). <code>CHECK</code> rejects rows where a boolean condition is false. <code>DEFAULT</code> supplies a value when an insert leaves the column out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0012","card":"q-sql-004-create-a-table-with-constraints","sec":"sql-s004","q":"What is the difference between <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> and <code>UNIQUE</code>?","a":"<p>A table has only one primary key, and its columns cannot be null. A table can have many <code>UNIQUE</code> constraints. In SQL Server a unique column may hold a single <code>NULL</code>; PostgreSQL and MySQL allow multiple. By default the primary key creates a clustered index in SQL Server and <code>UNIQUE</code> creates a non-clustered one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0013","card":"q-sql-004-create-a-table-with-constraints","sec":"sql-s004","q":"Does a <code>CHECK (quantity &gt; 0)</code> constraint reject a <code>NULL</code> quantity? Why or why not?","a":"<p>No. A <code>CHECK</code> rejects a row only when the condition evaluates to false. For <code>NULL</code> the result is unknown, which passes. If NULL must be rejected as well, add <code>NOT NULL</code> to the column.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0014","card":"q-sql-004-create-a-table-with-constraints","sec":"sql-s004","q":"When is a column's <code>DEFAULT</code> value used, and does inserting an explicit <code>NULL</code> use it?","a":"<p>The default applies when the column is left out of the insert column list or when you write the <code>DEFAULT</code> keyword. An explicit <code>NULL</code> is stored as <code>NULL</code> and does not trigger the default (unless the column is <code>NOT NULL</code>, in which case the insert fails).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0015","card":"q-sql-004-create-a-table-with-constraints","sec":"sql-s004","q":"Why should you name constraints (<code>CONSTRAINT ck_qty CHECK (...)</code>) instead of letting SQL Server name them?","a":"<p>Names the system picks automatically are random-looking, so scripts that later <code>ALTER TABLE ... DROP CONSTRAINT</code> cannot be reused across environments, and error messages are harder to read. Explicit names make migrations repeatable and errors clear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0016","card":"q-sql-005-insert-rows","sec":"sql-s005","q":"Why should an <code>INSERT</code> list its columns explicitly instead of relying on <code>INSERT INTO t VALUES (...)</code>?","a":"<p>Without a column list, values are matched by table column order. Adding or reordering a column later silently shifts the values or breaks the statement. An explicit list is safe against schema changes and shows which columns are skipped (identity, defaults, nullable).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0017","card":"q-sql-005-insert-rows","sec":"sql-s005","q":"How do you insert several rows in one statement, and why is it better than many single inserts?","a":"<p>Give several value tuples: <code>INSERT INTO t (a, b) VALUES (1,'x'), (2,'y'), (3,'z')</code>. It is one statement, one round trip and one transaction, so it is faster and atomic. SQL Server limits one <code>VALUES</code> list to 1000 rows. For larger loads use <code>INSERT ... SELECT</code> or bulk loading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0018","card":"q-sql-005-insert-rows","sec":"sql-s005","q":"What happens if you try to insert an explicit value into an <code>IDENTITY</code> column in SQL Server?","a":"<p>It fails with an error unless you run <code>SET IDENTITY_INSERT table ON</code> first (and then list the column explicitly). This is allowed for one table at a time per session, and is typically used for data migrations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0019","card":"q-sql-005-insert-rows","sec":"sql-s005","q":"How can you copy rows from one table into an existing table?","a":"<p>Use <code>INSERT INTO target (cols) SELECT cols FROM source WHERE ...</code>. The column count and compatible data types must match. To create a new table from a query instead, use <code>SELECT ... INTO</code> (SQL Server) or <code>CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT</code> (many other databases).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0020","card":"q-sql-006-display-records","sec":"sql-s006","q":"Why is <code>SELECT *</code> discouraged in production queries?","a":"<p>It returns every column, which wastes I/O and network, stops the optimizer from using a covering index, and breaks callers when columns are added or reordered. List only the columns you need. <code>SELECT *</code> is fine for quick exploration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0021","card":"q-sql-006-display-records","sec":"sql-s006","q":"In what logical order does SQL evaluate the clauses of a <code>SELECT</code>, and why does it matter?","a":"<p><code>FROM</code> (and joins), <code>WHERE</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>HAVING</code>, <code>SELECT</code>, <code>DISTINCT</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, then <code>TOP</code>/<code>LIMIT</code>. It explains why a column alias defined in <code>SELECT</code> cannot be used in <code>WHERE</code> or <code>HAVING</code> (in SQL Server) but can be used in <code>ORDER BY</code>, and why <code>WHERE</code> cannot filter on aggregates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0022","card":"q-sql-007-add-a-column-and-fill-it-with-update","sec":"sql-s007","q":"After <code>ALTER TABLE t ADD phone varchar(20)</code>, what do existing rows contain in the new column, and how do you fill it?","a":"<p><code>NULL</code>, because the column is nullable and no default exists. Fill it with <code>UPDATE t SET phone = ... WHERE ...</code>. If you add the column as <code>NOT NULL</code>, you must supply a <code>DEFAULT</code> so existing rows get a value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0023","card":"q-sql-007-add-a-column-and-fill-it-with-update","sec":"sql-s007","q":"What is the danger of running <code>UPDATE t SET col = 'x'</code> without a <code>WHERE</code> clause, and how do you protect yourself?","a":"<p>It changes every row. Protect yourself by running the same condition as a <code>SELECT</code> first, wrapping the change in <code>BEGIN TRAN</code> and checking the affected row count before <code>COMMIT</code> (or <code>ROLLBACK</code>), and using a backup or a restricted account in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0024","card":"q-sql-008-drop-a-column-change-a-data-type","sec":"sql-s008","q":"Which statements drop a column and change a column's data type in SQL Server?","a":"<p><code>ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN c</code> and <code>ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN c varchar(10)</code>. Other databases differ: MySQL uses <code>MODIFY COLUMN</code>, PostgreSQL uses <code>ALTER COLUMN c TYPE ...</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0025","card":"q-sql-008-drop-a-column-change-a-data-type","sec":"sql-s008","q":"In SQL Server, what are the risks of <code>ALTER COLUMN</code> to a smaller or different data type?","a":"<p>It fails if existing values do not fit or cannot convert (for example a 20-character string into <code>varchar(10)</code>), or if an index, constraint or default depends on the column. It can also lock the table while data is rewritten. You must restate <code>NOT NULL</code> if the column should stay non-nullable, otherwise it becomes nullable. Check the data first and test on a copy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0026","card":"q-sql-008-drop-a-column-change-a-data-type","sec":"sql-s008","q":"Why can <code>ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN</code> fail even if the column is empty?","a":"<p>A constraint (default, check, foreign key), index or schema-bound object depends on it. Drop the dependency first, then the column. Dropping a column is also irreversible without a backup, so confirm nothing in views, procedures or applications still uses it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0027","card":"q-sql-009-delete-records-and-drop-a-table","sec":"sql-s009","q":"Why can <code>DROP TABLE</code> fail on a table that other tables reference?","a":"<p>A foreign key constraint from another table depends on it. Drop or disable that constraint (or drop the child table first) before dropping the parent. This protects referential integrity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0028","card":"q-sql-010-create-the-database-and-tables","sec":"sql-s010","q":"How do you make one table reference another so that every order must belong to an existing customer?","a":"<p>Add a foreign key: <code>CustomerID int NOT NULL REFERENCES customer(CustomerID)</code> (or <code>FOREIGN KEY (CustomerID) REFERENCES customer(CustomerID)</code>). The database then rejects an insert with a missing customer and rejects deleting a customer that still has orders, unless you define <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> or similar.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0029","card":"q-sql-010-create-the-database-and-tables","sec":"sql-s010","q":"Why must you create the referenced (parent) tables before the table that has the foreign key?","a":"<p>The foreign key points to a primary key (or unique key) that must already exist. So create the parents first, and drop in the reverse order, children first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0030","card":"q-sql-010-create-the-database-and-tables","sec":"sql-s010","q":"Why use <code>tinyint</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>char(4)</code> or <code>varchar(50)</code> carefully when designing columns?","a":"<p>The type limits the valid range and sets storage size. <code>tinyint</code> holds only 0-255 in SQL Server, so an id column of that type will overflow at 256 rows. <code>char(n)</code> is fixed-length (padded) and suits fixed codes such as postcodes; <code>varchar(n)</code> is variable-length and suits names. Pick the smallest type that safely fits future growth.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0031","card":"q-sql-010-create-the-database-and-tables","sec":"sql-s010","q":"What does the <code>dbo.</code> prefix in <code>dbo.customer</code> mean in SQL Server?","a":"<p><code>dbo</code> is the default schema (the \"database owner\" schema). A schema is a namespace inside a database that groups objects and carries permissions. Qualifying names with a schema avoids ambiguity and a small name-resolution cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0032","card":"q-sql-011-check-which-tables-exist","sec":"sql-s011","q":"How can you list all tables in a database in a way that works on several SQL databases?","a":"<p>Query the ANSI <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA</code> views: <code>SELECT * FROM information_schema.tables</code> (add <code>WHERE table_type = 'BASE TABLE'</code> to exclude views). SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL support it. Each database also has its own catalog, for example <code>sys.tables</code> in SQL Server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0033","card":"q-sql-011-check-which-tables-exist","sec":"sql-s011","q":"What else can <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA</code> tell you besides table names?","a":"<p><code>information_schema.columns</code> gives column names, data types, nullability and defaults for every table. Others list constraints, views and routines. It is useful for scripts that inspect schema and for checking a table exists before dropping it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0034","card":"q-sql-012-top-order-by-and-distinct","sec":"sql-s012","q":"How do you return only the first 5 rows in SQL Server, MySQL/PostgreSQL, and standard SQL?","a":"<p>SQL Server: <code>SELECT TOP 5 * FROM t</code>. MySQL and PostgreSQL: <code>SELECT * FROM t LIMIT 5</code>. Standard SQL (SQL:2008; supported by PostgreSQL, Oracle 12c+ and SQL Server 2012+ with <code>ORDER BY</code>): <code>... ORDER BY col OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH FIRST 5 ROWS ONLY</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0035","card":"q-sql-012-top-order-by-and-distinct","sec":"sql-s012","q":"Why is <code>TOP</code> without <code>ORDER BY</code> unreliable?","a":"<p>A table has no guaranteed row order, so the engine returns whichever rows it reaches first. The result can change between runs. Use <code>ORDER BY</code> for a \"top N by something\" question, and add a tiebreaker column for a deterministic result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0036","card":"q-sql-012-top-order-by-and-distinct","sec":"sql-s012","q":"What does <code>TOP 40 PERCENT</code> return, and what does <code>TOP n WITH TIES</code> add?","a":"<p><code>TOP 40 PERCENT</code> returns 40% of the rows, rounded up to a whole row. <code>WITH TIES</code> (needs <code>ORDER BY</code>) also returns extra rows that tie with the last returned row on the sort value, so you can get more than n rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0037","card":"q-sql-012-top-order-by-and-distinct","sec":"sql-s012","q":"What is the default sort direction of <code>ORDER BY</code>, and how do you sort by several columns with mixed directions?","a":"<p>Ascending. List columns in priority order and set direction per column: <code>ORDER BY lastname DESC, firstname ASC</code>. <code>NULL</code> sorts first in ascending order in SQL Server (last in PostgreSQL and Oracle).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0038","card":"q-sql-012-top-order-by-and-distinct","sec":"sql-s012","q":"What is the problem with <code>ORDER BY 4, 2, 3 DESC</code> (column positions)?","a":"<p>It works, but it breaks silently when someone changes the select list, and it is hard to read. Use column names or aliases in production code. Positions are acceptable in quick ad hoc queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0039","card":"q-sql-012-top-order-by-and-distinct","sec":"sql-s012","q":"What does <code>SELECT DISTINCT lastname, firstname</code> de-duplicate: each column separately or the pair?","a":"<p>The combination. A row is removed only if the whole selected row matches an earlier one. <code>DISTINCT</code> is applied after the select list is computed and usually costs a sort or hash, so do not use it to hide an unintended join duplication.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0040","card":"q-sql-013-save-a-result-into-another-table","sec":"sql-s013","q":"What does <code>SELECT col INTO new_table FROM t</code> do in SQL Server, and what does it not copy?","a":"<p>It creates <code>new_table</code> with the column names and data types of the query, and fills it with the rows. It does not copy primary keys, foreign keys, indexes, check constraints or defaults. If the target exists already, the statement fails; use <code>INSERT ... SELECT</code> instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0041","card":"q-sql-013-save-a-result-into-another-table","sec":"sql-s013","q":"What is the equivalent of <code>SELECT ... INTO</code> in MySQL and PostgreSQL?","a":"<p><code>CREATE TABLE new_table AS SELECT ...</code>. <code>SELECT ... INTO</code> is T-SQL; in PostgreSQL <code>SELECT INTO</code> also exists but <code>CREATE TABLE AS</code> is the recommended form.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0042","card":"q-sql-013-save-a-result-into-another-table","sec":"sql-s013","q":"How do you create an empty copy of a table's column layout using <code>SELECT INTO</code>?","a":"<p>Add a condition that is never true: <code>SELECT * INTO copy FROM t WHERE 1 = 0</code>. The copy has the same columns and types but no rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0043","card":"q-sql-014-like-and-in","sec":"sql-s014","q":"What do <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> match in a <code>LIKE</code> pattern, and what does <code>LIKE '_r%'</code> return?","a":"<p><code>%</code> matches zero or more characters and <code>_</code> matches exactly one. <code>LIKE '_r%'</code> matches values whose second character is <code>r</code>, such as \"Brown\" or \"Crane\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0044","card":"q-sql-014-like-and-in","sec":"sql-s014","q":"Why can <code>WHERE name LIKE '%son'</code> be slow on a large table while <code>LIKE 'son%'</code> can be fast?","a":"<p>A leading wildcard means the engine cannot seek in an index on <code>name</code> and must scan every row. A pattern with a fixed prefix lets it seek the index range. For suffix or contains searches use full-text search or a different design.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0045","card":"q-sql-014-like-and-in","sec":"sql-s014","q":"Is <code>LIKE</code> case sensitive?","a":"<p>It depends on the column or database collation. SQL Server's default collation is case-insensitive, PostgreSQL's <code>LIKE</code> is case-sensitive (use <code>ILIKE</code>), and MySQL's depends on the collation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0046","card":"q-sql-014-like-and-in","sec":"sql-s014","q":"How do you search for a literal <code>%</code> or <code>_</code> with <code>LIKE</code>?","a":"<p>Escape it: <code>LIKE '%50\\%%' ESCAPE '\\'</code>. In SQL Server you can also wrap it in brackets, <code>LIKE '%[%]%'</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0047","card":"q-sql-014-like-and-in","sec":"sql-s014","q":"When do you prefer <code>IN (...)</code> over several <code>OR</code> conditions, and what is the <code>NOT IN</code> trap?","a":"<p><code>IN ('a','b','c')</code> is shorter and easier to read than <code>x='a' OR x='b' OR x='c'</code>, and has the same meaning. The trap is <code>NOT IN</code> with a list or subquery that contains <code>NULL</code>. The comparison becomes unknown for every row, so the query returns no rows. Filter the nulls out or use <code>NOT EXISTS</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0048","card":"q-sql-015-comparison-operators-and-null-checks","sec":"sql-s015","q":"Why does <code>WHERE col = NULL</code> return no rows, and what is the correct test?","a":"<p><code>NULL</code> means unknown, so <code>col = NULL</code> evaluates to unknown, not true. SQL uses three-valued logic (true, false, unknown) and <code>WHERE</code> keeps only true rows. Use <code>col IS NULL</code> or <code>col IS NOT NULL</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0049","card":"q-sql-015-comparison-operators-and-null-checks","sec":"sql-s015","q":"Does <code>WHERE lastname &lt;&gt; 'Brown'</code> return rows where <code>lastname</code> is NULL?","a":"<p>No. <code>NULL &lt;&gt; 'Brown'</code> is unknown, so those rows are dropped. To include them, write <code>WHERE lastname &lt;&gt; 'Brown' OR lastname IS NULL</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0050","card":"q-sql-015-comparison-operators-and-null-checks","sec":"sql-s015","q":"What is the difference between <code>&lt;&gt;</code> and <code>!=</code>?","a":"<p>Both mean \"not equal\". <code>&lt;&gt;</code> is standard SQL and works everywhere. <code>!=</code> is a common extension supported by SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL but not part of the standard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0051","card":"q-sql-015-comparison-operators-and-null-checks","sec":"sql-s015","q":"What does <code>WHERE lastname &gt; 'Brown'</code> mean for text columns?","a":"<p>Strings are compared by the column's collation order (roughly alphabetical), so it returns values that sort after \"Brown\". Case and accent handling depend on the collation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0052","card":"q-sql-016-between","sec":"sql-s016","q":"Are the end values included in <code>BETWEEN 5 AND 10</code>, and does the order of the two values matter?","a":"<p>Both ends are included, so it equals <code>&gt;= 5 AND &lt;= 10</code>. The lower value must come first: <code>BETWEEN 10 AND 5</code> returns no rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0053","card":"q-sql-016-between","sec":"sql-s016","q":"Why is <code>BETWEEN</code> risky for a <code>datetime</code> column, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p><code>BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-01-31'</code> treats the end as midnight at the start of Jan 31, so rows later that day are missed. Use a half-open range: <code>&gt;= '2024-01-01' AND &lt; '2024-02-01'</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0054","card":"q-sql-017-count-and-aliases","sec":"sql-s017","q":"What is the difference between <code>COUNT(*)</code>, <code>COUNT(col)</code> and <code>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code>?","a":"<p><code>COUNT(*)</code> counts all rows. <code>COUNT(col)</code> counts rows where <code>col</code> is not NULL. <code>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code> counts distinct non-NULL values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0055","card":"q-sql-017-count-and-aliases","sec":"sql-s017","q":"What does <code>AS</code> do, and where can you use an alias?","a":"<p>It gives a column or table a temporary name for that query. Column aliases appear in the result headers and can be used in <code>ORDER BY</code>, but not in <code>WHERE</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code> or <code>HAVING</code> in SQL Server, because those run before <code>SELECT</code>. Table aliases shorten joins and are required for self joins. In SQL Server, <code>[My Alias]</code> quotes names with spaces; the standard form is double quotes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0056","card":"q-sql-018-sum-with-group-by","sec":"sql-s018","q":"What does <code>GROUP BY</code> do, and what rule applies to the select list?","a":"<p>It collapses rows with the same grouping values into one row so aggregates (<code>SUM</code>, <code>COUNT</code>) are computed per group. Every selected column must be either in the <code>GROUP BY</code> or inside an aggregate function. Otherwise the query is invalid in SQL Server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0057","card":"q-sql-018-sum-with-group-by","sec":"sql-s018","q":"Why does this query fail, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p><code>name</code> is neither grouped nor aggregated, so the database cannot choose one value per department. Add <code>name</code> to <code>GROUP BY</code> (which changes the grouping to one row per department and name), remove it, or wrap it in an aggregate such as <code>MAX(name)</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT department, name, SUM(salary)\nFROM staff\nGROUP BY department</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"sql-f0058","card":"q-sql-018-sum-with-group-by","sec":"sql-s018","q":"In SQL Server, why does this query need <code>e.name</code> in the <code>GROUP BY</code> even though it groups by employee id?","a":"<p><code>e.name</code> is in <code>SELECT</code> without an aggregate, so SQL Server requires it in the group list, even though it depends functionally on the id. Add it to <code>GROUP BY</code> or wrap it in <code>MAX(e.name)</code>. PostgreSQL accepts the query when you group by the table's primary key, and MySQL does when <code>ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY</code> is off.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT e.id, e.name, SUM(s.qty)\nFROM employee e JOIN sales s ON s.emp_id = e.id\nGROUP BY e.id</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"sql-f0059","card":"q-sql-018-sum-with-group-by","sec":"sql-s018","q":"A query joins orders to employees and sums quantity per employee. What happens to the totals if the join duplicates rows?","a":"<p>Aggregates are computed on the joined rows, so duplication from a one-to-many join inflates <code>SUM</code> and <code>COUNT</code>. Aggregate in a subquery or CTE before joining, or join only on a unique key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0060","card":"q-sql-019-group-by-month","sec":"sql-s019","q":"What is wrong with <code>GROUP BY MONTH(order_date)</code> if the data covers several years?","a":"<p>January 2023 and January 2024 fall into the same group, so totals are mixed. Group by year and month: <code>GROUP BY YEAR(order_date), MONTH(order_date)</code>, or by a truncated date such as <code>DATETRUNC</code> (SQL Server 2022+) or <code>DATE_TRUNC</code> (PostgreSQL).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0061","card":"q-sql-019-group-by-month","sec":"sql-s019","q":"Why must the <code>GROUP BY</code> repeat the expression <code>month(saledate)</code> instead of using its alias?","a":"<p><code>GROUP BY</code> runs before <code>SELECT</code>, so the alias does not exist yet in SQL Server. You repeat the expression (or compute it in a subquery or CTE first). MySQL and PostgreSQL do allow aliases in <code>GROUP BY</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0062","card":"q-sql-020-max-min-and-avg","sec":"sql-s020","q":"How do <code>MAX</code>, <code>MIN</code> and <code>AVG</code> treat NULL values?","a":"<p>They ignore them. <code>AVG</code> divides by the number of non-NULL values, not by the row count. If every value is NULL, the result is NULL. To count NULL as zero, use <code>AVG(ISNULL(col, 0))</code> (or <code>COALESCE</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0063","card":"q-sql-020-max-min-and-avg","sec":"sql-s020","q":"In SQL Server, what does <code>AVG</code> return for an integer column with values 1 and 2, and how do you get the decimal result?","a":"<p>It returns 1, because the average of an <code>int</code> column is an <code>int</code> (truncated). Cast first: <code>AVG(CAST(col AS decimal(10,2)))</code> or <code>AVG(col * 1.0)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0064","card":"q-sql-020-max-min-and-avg","sec":"sql-s020","q":"How do you find the row that has the highest salary, not only the value?","a":"<p>Use a subquery: <code>WHERE salary = (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM t)</code>, which returns all ties. Or <code>SELECT TOP 1 ... ORDER BY salary DESC</code> for one row, or a ranking window function when you need ties or the nth highest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0065","card":"q-sql-021-having","sec":"sql-s021","q":"What is the difference between <code>WHERE</code> and <code>HAVING</code>?","a":"<p><code>WHERE</code> filters rows before grouping, so it cannot use aggregates. <code>HAVING</code> filters groups after aggregation, so it can (<code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>). Put non-aggregate conditions in <code>WHERE</code>, because filtering early means fewer rows to group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0066","card":"q-sql-021-having","sec":"sql-s021","q":"Given a table <code>emp(JobTitle, ...)</code> with one row per employee, how do you list the job titles held by more than one employee?","a":"<p>Group by job and filter the groups: <code>SELECT JobTitle, COUNT(*) FROM emp GROUP BY JobTitle HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>. <code>HAVING</code> is needed because the condition is on an aggregate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0067","card":"q-sql-021-having","sec":"sql-s021","q":"Why does <code>HAVING AVG(Salary) &gt; 45000</code> work, but <code>HAVING AvgSal &gt; 45000</code> fail in SQL Server when <code>AvgSal</code> is the alias of that average?","a":"<p><code>HAVING</code> is evaluated before <code>SELECT</code>, so the alias is not yet defined. Repeat the aggregate expression or use a subquery or CTE.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0068","card":"q-sql-021-having","sec":"sql-s021","q":"Can you use <code>HAVING</code> without <code>GROUP BY</code>?","a":"<p>Yes. The whole table is treated as one group, so <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 10</code> returns a row only if the count exceeds 10. It is rare in practice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0069","card":"q-sql-022-cast-and-convert","sec":"sql-s022","q":"How do <code>CAST</code> and <code>CONVERT</code> differ, and which one do you pick?","a":"<p><code>CAST(expr AS type)</code> is ANSI standard and portable. <code>CONVERT(type, expr, style)</code> is T-SQL only, but its <code>style</code> argument controls formatting, for example date-to-string formats. Use <code>CAST</code> by default, and <code>CONVERT</code> when you need a style.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0070","card":"q-sql-022-cast-and-convert","sec":"sql-s022","q":"Does <code>CAST</code> change the stored data type of the column?","a":"<p>No. It changes the type of the value for that query only. To change the column permanently use <code>ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0071","card":"q-sql-022-cast-and-convert","sec":"sql-s022","q":"What happens when <code>CAST('abc' AS int)</code> runs, and what is the safer alternative in SQL Server?","a":"<p>It raises a conversion error and stops the query. <code>TRY_CAST('abc' AS int)</code> (SQL Server 2012+) returns NULL instead, so one bad row does not fail the whole query. <code>TRY_CONVERT</code> is the same for <code>CONVERT</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0072","card":"q-sql-022-cast-and-convert","sec":"sql-s022","q":"Why is comparing a column cast to another type in <code>WHERE</code> (for example <code>WHERE CAST(col AS date) = '2024-01-01'</code>) a performance risk?","a":"<p>Applying a function to the column can prevent an index seek, so the engine scans the table. Rewrite as a range on the raw column. Note that SQL Server specifically optimizes <code>CAST(datetime AS date)</code>, but other expressions are not safe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0073","card":"q-sql-023-case-statement","sec":"sql-s023","q":"How does a <code>CASE</code> expression evaluate, and what happens if no branch matches and there is no <code>ELSE</code>?","a":"<p>Conditions are evaluated top to bottom and the first true <code>WHEN</code> wins, so order matters (put the most specific first). If none match and there is no <code>ELSE</code>, the result is <code>NULL</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0074","card":"q-sql-023-case-statement","sec":"sql-s023","q":"What is the difference between searched <code>CASE WHEN age &gt; 30 THEN ...</code> and simple <code>CASE age WHEN 30 THEN ...</code>?","a":"<p>A simple <code>CASE</code> only tests equality of one expression against listed values. A searched <code>CASE</code> accepts any boolean condition, such as ranges and <code>IS NULL</code>. The searched form is more flexible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0075","card":"q-sql-023-case-statement","sec":"sql-s023","q":"Why does this <code>CASE</code> return 'Baby' for a row whose Age is NULL?","a":"<p>Comparisons with NULL are unknown, so both <code>WHEN</code> conditions fail and the <code>ELSE</code> branch runs. Add an explicit <code>WHEN Age IS NULL THEN ...</code> or filter NULLs out first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT Age,\nCASE WHEN Age &gt; 30 THEN &#x27;Old&#x27;\n     WHEN Age BETWEEN 27 AND 30 THEN &#x27;Young&#x27;\n     ELSE &#x27;Baby&#x27;\nEND\nFROM people</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"sql-f0076","card":"q-sql-023-case-statement","sec":"sql-s023","q":"Name three common uses of <code>CASE</code> besides labelling values.","a":"<p>Conditional calculations (different raise percentage per job title), pivoting with conditional aggregates (<code>SUM(CASE WHEN status='open' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)</code>), and custom sort order (<code>ORDER BY CASE ...</code>). It can be used in <code>SELECT</code>, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code> and <code>GROUP BY</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0077","card":"q-sql-023-case-statement","sec":"sql-s023","q":"What restriction applies to the data types of the <code>THEN</code> and <code>ELSE</code> results of a <code>CASE</code>?","a":"<p>All branches must resolve to one result type. SQL Server picks the type with the highest precedence, so mixing text and numbers can cause a conversion error. Convert branches explicitly to the same type.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0078","card":"q-sql-024-partition-by","sec":"sql-s024","q":"How does <code>OVER (PARTITION BY ...)</code> differ from <code>GROUP BY</code>?","a":"<p><code>GROUP BY</code> collapses rows, returning one per group. A window function with <code>PARTITION BY</code> keeps every row and adds the aggregate for the row's partition as an extra column. So you can show detail and group totals side by side, for example each employee with the headcount for their gender.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0079","card":"q-sql-024-partition-by","sec":"sql-s024","q":"What does <code>COUNT(Gender) OVER (PARTITION BY Gender)</code> return for each row?","a":"<p>The number of rows (with non-NULL Gender) that share that row's Gender value. Every row in the same group has the same value, and all the detail columns are still available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0080","card":"q-sql-024-partition-by","sec":"sql-s024","q":"What does an empty <code>OVER ()</code> mean?","a":"<p>The window is the whole result set, so <code>AVG(Salary) OVER ()</code> puts the overall average on every row. It is the window version of a scalar subquery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0081","card":"q-sql-024-partition-by","sec":"sql-s024","q":"Why can't you use a window function in a <code>WHERE</code> clause, and how do you filter on its result?","a":"<p>Window functions are computed after <code>WHERE</code> has run (they belong to the <code>SELECT</code> stage). Put the query in a CTE or derived table and filter in the outer query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0082","card":"q-sql-025-string-functions-trim-and-replace","sec":"sql-s025","q":"What do <code>LTRIM</code>, <code>RTRIM</code> and <code>TRIM</code> do, and which SQL Server version added <code>TRIM</code>?","a":"<p><code>LTRIM</code> removes leading spaces, <code>RTRIM</code> removes trailing spaces, and <code>TRIM</code> removes both. <code>TRIM</code> was added in SQL Server 2017; in earlier versions use <code>LTRIM(RTRIM(col))</code>. From 2017 <code>TRIM</code> can also remove other listed characters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0083","card":"q-sql-025-string-functions-trim-and-replace","sec":"sql-s025","q":"What does <code>REPLACE(LastName, '- Fired', '')</code> do, and what if the text appears several times?","a":"<p>It replaces every occurrence of the search text with the replacement; an empty string deletes it. Matching is case-insensitive or case-sensitive depending on the collation. It does not change the table unless used in an <code>UPDATE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0084","card":"q-sql-025-string-functions-trim-and-replace","sec":"sql-s025","q":"Why might <code>WHERE name = 'Tom'</code> miss a row that looks like 'Tom ' and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>Hidden leading spaces (or other characters) make values differ. Clean with <code>TRIM</code>, or fix the data once with <code>UPDATE t SET name = TRIM(name)</code>. Note that SQL Server ignores trailing spaces in <code>=</code> comparisons but not leading ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0085","card":"q-sql-026-string-functions-substring-upper-and-low","sec":"sql-s026","q":"What are the arguments of <code>SUBSTRING</code> in SQL Server, and does it start counting at 0 or 1?","a":"<p><code>SUBSTRING(string, start, length)</code>. Positions start at 1. <code>SUBSTRING('Hello', 1, 3)</code> returns <code>Hel</code>. All three arguments are required in T-SQL (MySQL and PostgreSQL allow omitting length).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0086","card":"q-sql-026-string-functions-substring-upper-and-low","sec":"sql-s026","q":"Why are <code>UPPER</code> and <code>LOWER</code> used when comparing text?","a":"<p>To make a comparison case-insensitive on a case-sensitive collation: <code>WHERE UPPER(a) = UPPER(b)</code>. The cost is that wrapping an indexed column in a function usually prevents an index seek. On a case-insensitive collation it is unnecessary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0087","card":"q-sql-026-string-functions-substring-upper-and-low","sec":"sql-s026","q":"What is the performance problem with joining on <code>SUBSTRING(a.col,1,3) = SUBSTRING(b.col,1,3)</code>?","a":"<p>A function on both join columns hides them from any index, so the join becomes a scan (a nested loop or hash over all rows). It is acceptable for one-off data cleaning, but for regular queries store a computed or persisted column and index it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0088","card":"q-sql-027-create-and-run-a-stored-procedure","sec":"sql-s027","q":"What are the benefits of a stored procedure compared with sending ad hoc SQL from an application?","a":"<p>Logic lives once in the database and is reusable. Parameters keep values separate from SQL text, which prevents SQL injection. Execution plans are cached and reused. Permissions can be granted on the procedure without granting access to the underlying tables. Fewer round trips for multi-statement logic. The cost is that logic is harder to version, test and port than application code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0089","card":"q-sql-027-create-and-run-a-stored-procedure","sec":"sql-s027","q":"How do you define and call a stored procedure with a parameter in SQL Server?","a":"<p>Define: <code>CREATE PROCEDURE dbo.GetByJob @JobTitle nvarchar(100) AS SELECT ... WHERE JobTitle = @JobTitle</code>. Call: <code>EXEC dbo.GetByJob @JobTitle = 'Salesman'</code>. The procedure is stored after creation, so later you only run <code>EXEC</code>. To change it, use <code>ALTER PROCEDURE</code> (or <code>CREATE OR ALTER</code>, SQL Server 2016 SP1+).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0090","card":"q-sql-027-create-and-run-a-stored-procedure","sec":"sql-s027","q":"What does <code>GO</code> do between a procedure definition and <code>EXEC</code>, and is it T-SQL?","a":"<p><code>GO</code> is not a T-SQL statement. It is a batch separator understood by tools such as SSMS and <code>sqlcmd</code>. <code>CREATE PROCEDURE</code> must be the only statement in its batch, so <code>GO</code> ends the procedure body before the <code>EXEC</code> batch begins. Without it, the <code>EXEC</code> line would become part of the procedure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0091","card":"q-sql-027-create-and-run-a-stored-procedure","sec":"sql-s027","q":"Why use a parameter in the <code>WHERE</code> clause instead of building the SQL text with string concatenation?","a":"<p>A parameter is treated as data and cannot change the query structure, so SQL injection is not possible. It also allows plan reuse. String concatenation with user input lets an attacker alter the query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0092","card":"q-sql-028-subquery-in-select-and-with-partition-by","sec":"sql-s028","q":"What rule must a subquery in the <code>SELECT</code> list follow, and what error do you get if you break it?","a":"<p>It must be scalar: one column and at most one row. If it returns more than one row, SQL Server raises \"Subquery returned more than 1 value\". If it returns no rows, the result is NULL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0093","card":"q-sql-028-subquery-in-select-and-with-partition-by","sec":"sql-s028","q":"What is the difference between a correlated and a non-correlated subquery?","a":"<p>A non-correlated subquery does not reference the outer query, so it can run once (such as <code>SELECT AVG(Salary) FROM t</code>). A correlated subquery refers to the outer row and is conceptually evaluated per row, which can be slow, though the optimizer often rewrites it as a join.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0094","card":"q-sql-028-subquery-in-select-and-with-partition-by","sec":"sql-s028","q":"Two ways to add the overall average salary to every row: <code>(SELECT AVG(Salary) FROM t)</code> and <code>AVG(Salary) OVER ()</code>. How do they differ?","a":"<p>Both give the same value on every row. The window form is shorter and can be partitioned (<code>OVER (PARTITION BY dept)</code>) to get per-group averages, which the plain scalar subquery can only do with a correlated condition. The scalar subquery form works on databases without window functions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0095","card":"q-sql-029-subquery-in-from","sec":"sql-s029","q":"What is a subquery in the <code>FROM</code> clause called, and why does it need an alias?","a":"<p>A derived table. The outer query treats it like a table, so it needs a name to refer to its columns. SQL Server requires the alias (<code>... ) a</code>). PostgreSQL required it before version 16 and makes it optional from 16.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0096","card":"q-sql-030-subquery-in-where","sec":"sql-s030","q":"When do you use <code>IN (subquery)</code> and when <code>= (subquery)</code>?","a":"<p><code>IN</code> is for a subquery that may return many values. <code>=</code> requires a single value, and errors if the subquery returns more than one row. For \"salary equals the maximum\", <code>= (SELECT MAX(salary) ...)</code> is safe because an aggregate returns one row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0097","card":"q-sql-030-subquery-in-where","sec":"sql-s030","q":"What is a subquery with <code>EXISTS</code>, and how does it compare with <code>IN</code>?","a":"<p><code>EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM b WHERE b.id = a.id)</code> returns true when the subquery returns at least one row, and stops at the first match. It is null-safe, while <code>NOT IN</code> is not. For large lists the optimizer often produces similar plans, but <code>NOT EXISTS</code> is usually the safer anti-join form.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0098","card":"q-sql-030-subquery-in-where","sec":"sql-s030","q":"How would you find employees whose department has an average salary higher than the company average, in terms of subquery placement?","a":"<p>Compute the group average in a subquery grouped by department, compare it to a scalar subquery of the company average with <code>HAVING</code>, then filter employees with <code>WHERE dept_id IN (...)</code>. It combines subqueries in <code>HAVING</code> and <code>WHERE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0099","card":"q-sql-031-join-types","sec":"sql-s031","q":"What are the main join types and what does each return?","a":"<p><code>INNER JOIN</code>: only rows with a match in both tables. <code>LEFT JOIN</code>: all rows from the left, with NULLs for the right when there is no match. <code>RIGHT JOIN</code>: the mirror image. <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code>: all rows from both sides, with NULLs where there is no match. <code>CROSS JOIN</code>: every combination (Cartesian product). A self join joins a table to itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0100","card":"q-sql-031-join-types","sec":"sql-s031","q":"How do you choose between an inner join and a left join?","a":"<p>Use inner join when you only want rows that exist in both tables. Use left join when you must keep every row of the main table even if related data is missing, for example customers with no orders.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0101","card":"q-sql-031-join-types","sec":"sql-s031","q":"Which clause defines how two tables are matched in a join, and is it always required?","a":"<p>The <code>ON</code> clause. It is mandatory with <code>INNER</code>, <code>LEFT</code>, <code>RIGHT</code> and <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code>. <code>CROSS JOIN</code> has no <code>ON</code> clause because it intentionally pairs every row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0102","card":"q-sql-032-explicit-join-comma-syntax-no-join-keywo","sec":"sql-s032","q":"What is the difference between <code>FROM a, b WHERE a.id = b.id</code> and <code>FROM a JOIN b ON a.id = b.id</code>?","a":"<p>Both give the same inner join result. The comma form is the older ANSI-89 syntax with the join condition in <code>WHERE</code>. The <code>JOIN ... ON</code> form (ANSI-92) separates join conditions from filters, is easier to read, and makes outer joins possible with clear syntax. Prefer <code>JOIN</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0103","card":"q-sql-032-explicit-join-comma-syntax-no-join-keywo","sec":"sql-s032","q":"What is the danger of the comma syntax when you forget the <code>WHERE</code> condition?","a":"<p>The query silently returns a Cartesian product (every row of one table combined with every row of the other), which can be huge and slow. The <code>JOIN</code> syntax makes you write <code>ON</code>, so you notice the omission.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0104","card":"q-sql-033-inner-join-join-keyword","sec":"sql-s033","q":"Is <code>JOIN</code> the same as <code>INNER JOIN</code>?","a":"<p>Yes. <code>INNER</code> is optional. A bare <code>JOIN</code> is an inner join.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0105","card":"q-sql-033-inner-join-join-keyword","sec":"sql-s033","q":"What happens to the row count when you inner join a parent table to a child table with many rows per parent?","a":"<p>Each parent row repeats once per matching child row (fan-out), and parents without children disappear. So aggregates over the parent's columns can be inflated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0106","card":"q-sql-033-inner-join-join-keyword","sec":"sql-s033","q":"Does it matter whether a join condition is in <code>ON</code> or in <code>WHERE</code> for an inner join?","a":"<p>No, results are the same for an inner join and the optimizer treats them identically. For outer joins it matters a lot, see left join. Keep join conditions in <code>ON</code> and filters in <code>WHERE</code> for readability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0107","card":"q-sql-033-inner-join-join-keyword","sec":"sql-s033","q":"How do you join three tables?","a":"<p>Chain joins: <code>FROM customer c JOIN sale s ON c.id = s.customer_id JOIN inventory i ON s.inventory_id = i.id</code>. Each join links the next table to one already in the query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0108","card":"q-sql-034-left-join","sec":"sql-s034","q":"How do you find rows in table A that have no match in table B?","a":"<p>Left join and filter for NULL on the right table's key: <code>FROM A LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.a_id WHERE B.a_id IS NULL</code>. It is called an anti join. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> and <code>NOT IN</code> do the same.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0109","card":"q-sql-034-left-join","sec":"sql-s034","q":"Why test a key column of the right table with <code>IS NULL</code> instead of any column?","a":"<p>The key is not nullable in the right table, so NULL can only mean \"no matching row\". A nullable column could be NULL even in a matched row and give false positives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0110","card":"q-sql-034-left-join","sec":"sql-s034","q":"What is the difference between putting a right-table filter in <code>ON</code> versus <code>WHERE</code> in a left join?","a":"<p>A condition on the right table in <code>ON</code> limits which rows match, and left rows are still kept with NULLs. The same condition in <code>WHERE</code> removes the NULL-extended rows, so the left join behaves like an inner join. Use <code>ON</code> to filter the right side while keeping all left rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0111","card":"q-sql-034-left-join","sec":"sql-s034","q":"For finding unmatched rows, how do <code>LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL</code>, <code>NOT IN</code> and <code>NOT EXISTS</code> compare?","a":"<p>All can express it. <code>NOT IN</code> returns nothing if the subquery produces a NULL, so it is the riskiest. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> is null-safe and usually efficient. <code>LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL</code> is null-safe and readable, and gives access to left columns only. Pick <code>NOT EXISTS</code> or the left join.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0112","card":"q-sql-035-right-join-and-full-outer-join","sec":"sql-s035","q":"When would you use a <code>RIGHT JOIN</code>, and can you always rewrite it?","a":"<p>Rarely. A right join keeps all rows of the right table, and swapping the table order turns it into a left join with the same result. Most teams standardize on left joins for consistent reading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0113","card":"q-sql-035-right-join-and-full-outer-join","sec":"sql-s035","q":"What does <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> return, and how do you find rows that exist in only one of the two tables?","a":"<p>All rows from both tables, matched where possible and NULL-extended where not. To get the mismatches only, add <code>WHERE a.key IS NULL OR b.key IS NULL</code>. It is useful for comparing two data sets, such as reconciliation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0114","card":"q-sql-035-right-join-and-full-outer-join","sec":"sql-s035","q":"Does MySQL support <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code>?","a":"<p>No. MySQL does not have it. Emulate with <code>LEFT JOIN ... UNION ... RIGHT JOIN</code>. SQL Server and PostgreSQL support it directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0115","card":"q-sql-036-self-join","sec":"sql-s036","q":"What is a self join and when do you need it?","a":"<p>A table joined to itself, using two aliases to treat it as two tables. Typical cases: employee-manager hierarchies (<code>managerID</code> refers to <code>employeeID</code>), comparing rows within a table (consecutive events, finding pairs), and finding duplicates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0116","card":"q-sql-036-self-join","sec":"sql-s036","q":"Why are table aliases mandatory in a self join?","a":"<p>Both sides have the same table name, so the database cannot tell which column belongs to which role. Aliases such as <code>E</code> (employee) and <code>M</code> (manager) make the references unambiguous.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0117","card":"q-sql-036-self-join","sec":"sql-s036","q":"In an employee-manager self join with an inner join, what happens to the top manager and why?","a":"<p>The top manager has a NULL <code>managerID</code>, which matches nothing, so the row is dropped from an inner join result. Use a left join to keep it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0118","card":"q-sql-036-self-join","sec":"sql-s036","q":"What limits a self join for hierarchies, and what is the alternative for arbitrary depth?","a":"<p>One self join walks only one level (employee to direct manager). Each extra level needs another join, which is impractical for unknown depth. Use a recursive CTE instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0119","card":"q-sql-036-self-join","sec":"sql-s036","q":"How do you build a \"Full Name\" from first and last name columns in SQL Server, and what happens with NULL parts?","a":"<p><code>first + ' ' + last</code>. With the default setting, concatenating NULL with <code>+</code> gives NULL, so one NULL part makes the whole name NULL. <code>CONCAT(first, ' ', last)</code> (SQL Server 2012+) treats NULL as an empty string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0120","card":"q-sql-037-self-join-with-left-outer-join","sec":"sql-s037","q":"How do you list all employees with their manager's name, including the employee who has no manager?","a":"<p>Self join with <code>LEFT JOIN</code>: <code>FROM staff E LEFT JOIN staff M ON E.managerID = M.employeeID</code>. The top person is kept and gets NULL for the manager columns. You can show a label with <code>ISNULL(M.name, 'No manager')</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0121","card":"q-sql-037-self-join-with-left-outer-join","sec":"sql-s037","q":"To list every employee with the manager's name from one <code>staff</code> table (<code>employeeID</code>, <code>managerID</code>), the query is <code>FROM staff E LEFT JOIN staff M ON E.managerID = M.employeeID</code>. What changes if you swap the aliases so <code>M</code> is on the left?","a":"<p>Both aliases read the same table, so no rows are lost, but the meaning changes. The left side is now the person you keep, joined to the rows that have that person as <code>managerID</code>. The result becomes one row per manager and report pair. Managers repeat once per report, and people with no reports (leaf employees) appear with NULL in the reports column. It no longer gives one row per employee with a manager name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0122","card":"q-sql-038-cross-join","sec":"sql-s038","q":"What does <code>CROSS JOIN</code> return, and how many rows?","a":"<p>Every combination of a row from the first table with a row from the second. Row count is m x n, so 100 x 1000 gives 100,000 rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0123","card":"q-sql-038-cross-join","sec":"sql-s038","q":"When is a cross join the right tool?","a":"<p>When you want all combinations deliberately: sizes x colors for a product catalogue, dates x stores for a report grid, or a calendar or number sequence. It is usually a bug when it appears by accident from a missing join condition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0124","card":"q-sql-039-union-and-union-all","sec":"sql-s039","q":"What is the difference between <code>UNION</code> and <code>UNION ALL</code>, and which one is faster?","a":"<p><code>UNION</code> combines results and removes duplicate rows. <code>UNION ALL</code> combines and keeps everything. <code>UNION ALL</code> is faster because it skips the sort or hash needed to find duplicates. Use it when duplicates are impossible or acceptable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0125","card":"q-sql-039-union-and-union-all","sec":"sql-s039","q":"What are the requirements for combining two queries with a set operator?","a":"<p>Both queries must return the same number of columns, with compatible data types in matching positions. Column names come from the first query. No common key is needed. <code>ORDER BY</code> is allowed only once, at the end, and sorts the combined result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0126","card":"q-sql-039-union-and-union-all","sec":"sql-s039","q":"How does <code>UNION</code> compare with a join?","a":"<p>A join places columns from two tables side by side, matching rows by key. <code>UNION</code> stacks rows from queries with the same shape on top of each other. Use a join to enrich rows and <code>UNION</code> to merge similar data sets (for example sales from two regions).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0127","card":"q-sql-039-union-and-union-all","sec":"sql-s039","q":"Does <code>UNION</code> treat two rows with NULL in the same column as duplicates?","a":"<p>Yes. Set operators treat NULLs as equal for duplicate removal, unlike the <code>=</code> operator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0128","card":"q-sql-040-intersect","sec":"sql-s040","q":"What does <code>INTERSECT</code> return, and does it keep duplicates?","a":"<p>The distinct rows that appear in both query results. Duplicates are removed. Column count and types must match, as with <code>UNION</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0129","card":"q-sql-040-intersect","sec":"sql-s040","q":"Is <code>INTERSECT</code> always the same as an inner join on the same columns?","a":"<p>No. An inner join returns one row per matching pair, so duplicates in either table multiply, while <code>INTERSECT</code> returns each common row once. Also, <code>INTERSECT</code> treats NULLs as equal, but a join on <code>=</code> does not match NULLs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0130","card":"q-sql-040-intersect","sec":"sql-s040","q":"Which databases support <code>INTERSECT</code>?","a":"<p>SQL Server, PostgreSQL and Oracle support it. MySQL added <code>INTERSECT</code> and <code>EXCEPT</code> in version 8.0.31; earlier MySQL needs a join or <code>IN</code>/<code>EXISTS</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0131","card":"q-sql-040-intersect","sec":"sql-s040","q":"What is the precedence between <code>INTERSECT</code> and <code>UNION</code> when you chain them?","a":"<p>In standard SQL and SQL Server, <code>INTERSECT</code> binds tighter than <code>UNION</code> and <code>EXCEPT</code>. Use parentheses to make the intent explicit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0132","card":"q-sql-041-except","sec":"sql-s041","q":"What does <code>A EXCEPT B</code> return, and does the order of the queries matter?","a":"<p>Distinct rows from A that are not in B. Order matters: <code>B EXCEPT A</code> gives a different result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0133","card":"q-sql-041-except","sec":"sql-s041","q":"What is the Oracle equivalent of <code>EXCEPT</code>?","a":"<p><code>MINUS</code>. PostgreSQL and SQL Server use <code>EXCEPT</code>; MySQL supports <code>EXCEPT</code> from 8.0.31.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0134","card":"q-sql-041-except","sec":"sql-s041","q":"Is <code>WHERE last NOT IN (SELECT last FROM b) AND first NOT IN (SELECT first FROM b)</code> equivalent to <code>EXCEPT</code> on both columns?","a":"<p>No. It checks each column independently, so a person is dropped if the last name appears anywhere in B or the first name appears anywhere in B, even when they belong to different people. <code>EXCEPT</code> compares whole rows. The correct anti-join is <code>NOT EXISTS</code> with both columns matched in one correlated condition. <code>NOT IN</code> also returns no rows when B contains NULLs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0135","card":"q-sql-042-view","sec":"sql-s042","q":"What is a view, and does it store data?","a":"<p>A view is a saved <code>SELECT</code> that you query like a table. A normal view stores only the query, not data, so it always shows the current base table data. An indexed (materialized) view stores its result physically and is refreshed automatically in SQL Server, or manually in PostgreSQL (<code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0136","card":"q-sql-042-view","sec":"sql-s042","q":"Why use a view?","a":"<p>To hide complex joins behind a simple name, to expose only selected columns or rows for security, to give a stable interface when the underlying tables change, and to reuse logic. The cost is that nesting views can hide expensive queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0137","card":"q-sql-042-view","sec":"sql-s042","q":"Can you insert or update through a view?","a":"<p>Only for simple views that map to a single base table without aggregates, <code>DISTINCT</code> or <code>GROUP BY</code> (and joins have limits). Otherwise use <code>INSTEAD OF</code> triggers. Rows changed through a view change the base table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0138","card":"q-sql-042-view","sec":"sql-s042","q":"When do you choose a view over a temp table or CTE?","a":"<p>A view is permanent and shared by many queries and users, with permissions of its own. A CTE exists for one statement. A temp table exists for a session. Pick a view when the logic is reused across queries over time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0139","card":"q-sql-042-view","sec":"sql-s042","q":"Does <code>ORDER BY</code> work inside a SQL Server view definition?","a":"<p>Not unless the view also uses <code>TOP</code>. Even then, a view is not a sorted object, so sort when you select from the view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0140","card":"q-sql-043-temp-table","sec":"sql-s043","q":"What does a leading <code>#</code> mean on a SQL Server table name, and how does <code>##</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>#name</code> is a local temp table, visible only to the session that created it and dropped when the session ends (or when the procedure that created it ends). <code>##name</code> is global, visible to all sessions and dropped when the creating session ends and no one is using it. Both live in <code>tempdb</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0141","card":"q-sql-043-temp-table","sec":"sql-s043","q":"When do you use a temp table instead of a CTE or a subquery?","a":"<p>When you reuse an intermediate result several times, when it is large and benefits from an index or statistics, or when you break a complex query into steps. A CTE is just a named query that is re-evaluated at each reference, and cannot be indexed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0142","card":"q-sql-043-temp-table","sec":"sql-s043","q":"Is a temp table updated when the base table changes?","a":"<p>No. It holds a snapshot of the data at the moment it was filled. A view or CTE always reads current data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0143","card":"q-sql-043-temp-table","sec":"sql-s043","q":"What is the difference between a temp table and a table variable (<code>DECLARE @t TABLE</code>)?","a":"<p>A temp table supports indexes and statistics, can be altered, and participates in transaction rollback. A table variable is scoped to the batch, has limited statistics (in older versions the optimizer assumes one row), and is not affected by transaction rollback. Prefer temp tables for larger data, table variables for small sets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0144","card":"q-sql-043-temp-table","sec":"sql-s043","q":"Why start a temp table script with <code>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS #t</code>?","a":"<p>So the script can be run repeatedly in one session without a \"table already exists\" error. <code>IF EXISTS</code> needs SQL Server 2016+; before that use <code>IF OBJECT_ID('tempdb..#t') IS NOT NULL DROP TABLE #t</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0145","card":"q-sql-044-cte-common-table-expression","sec":"sql-s044","q":"What is a CTE and what are its benefits?","a":"<p>A named temporary result set defined with <code>WITH name AS (SELECT ...)</code> and used by the statement that follows. It makes complex queries readable by breaking them into named steps, can be referenced multiple times in the same statement, and supports recursion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0146","card":"q-sql-044-cte-common-table-expression","sec":"sql-s044","q":"What is the scope of a CTE?","a":"<p>One statement only (the <code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> right after it). It is not stored, cannot be indexed, and disappears when the statement ends. Use a temp table or view to reuse across statements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0147","card":"q-sql-044-cte-common-table-expression","sec":"sql-s044","q":"Why does SQL Server require a semicolon before <code>WITH</code> in a CTE that follows another statement?","a":"<p><code>WITH</code> is also used for table hints, so the previous statement must be terminated with <code>;</code> to avoid ambiguity. A common habit is to write <code>;WITH cte AS (...)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0148","card":"q-sql-044-cte-common-table-expression","sec":"sql-s044","q":"What is a recursive CTE and what are its two parts?","a":"<p>A CTE that references itself, used for hierarchies and sequences. It has an anchor member (the starting rows) combined with <code>UNION ALL</code> to a recursive member (that joins to the CTE itself). It stops when the recursive part returns no rows. SQL Server limits depth to 100 by default (<code>OPTION (MAXRECURSION n)</code>; 0 means no limit).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0149","card":"q-sql-044-cte-common-table-expression","sec":"sql-s044","q":"Compare CTE and subquery in <code>FROM</code>.","a":"<p>They give the same result for a single use. A CTE is named and can be referenced several times and read top to bottom, so it is more readable. Neither is materialized by default in SQL Server. PostgreSQL 12+ inlines CTEs unless they are recursive, have side effects or are marked <code>MATERIALIZED</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0150","card":"q-sql-045-duplicate-a-table","sec":"sql-s045","q":"Does <code>ORDER BY</code> in a <code>SELECT ... INTO</code> guarantee the stored row order?","a":"<p>No. Tables have no inherent order. The <code>ORDER BY</code> only affects the order in which identity values (if any) are assigned. Always sort when reading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0151","card":"q-sql-046-row-number","sec":"sql-s046","q":"What does <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY Salary DESC)</code> return, and what happens with ties?","a":"<p>A unique sequential number 1, 2, 3, ... per row in the sort order. Rows with equal salaries still get different numbers, and which gets the lower number is arbitrary unless you add a tiebreaker column to <code>ORDER BY</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0152","card":"q-sql-046-row-number","sec":"sql-s046","q":"How do you get the top 2 earners in each department?","a":"<p>Number rows within each department and filter in an outer query: <code>WITH r AS (SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) rn FROM emp) SELECT * FROM r WHERE rn &lt;= 2</code>. Use <code>RANK</code> or <code>DENSE_RANK</code> instead when ties should all be included.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0153","card":"q-sql-046-row-number","sec":"sql-s046","q":"Is <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER()</code> required for <code>ROW_NUMBER</code>?","a":"<p>Yes, in SQL Server ranking functions need <code>ORDER BY</code> in the <code>OVER</code> clause, because numbering needs a defined order. <code>PARTITION BY</code> is optional.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0154","card":"q-sql-046-row-number","sec":"sql-s046","q":"How do you do pagination with <code>ROW_NUMBER</code>, and what is the modern alternative?","a":"<p>Number the rows and select a range (<code>WHERE rn BETWEEN 21 AND 30</code>). In SQL Server 2012+ use <code>ORDER BY ... OFFSET 20 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY</code>. For deep pages, keyset pagination (<code>WHERE id &gt; last_id</code>) is faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0155","card":"q-sql-047-rank","sec":"sql-s047","q":"What does <code>RANK()</code> do with ties, and what is the next rank after a tie?","a":"<p>Tied rows get the same rank, and the next rank skips numbers. For salaries 100, 90, 90, 80 the ranks are 1, 2, 2, 4.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0156","card":"q-sql-047-rank","sec":"sql-s047","q":"What does <code>PARTITION BY</code> add to <code>RANK()</code>?","a":"<p>It restarts the ranking for each group. <code>RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY JobTitle ORDER BY Salary DESC)</code> ranks salaries within each job title separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0157","card":"q-sql-047-rank","sec":"sql-s047","q":"When do you choose <code>RANK</code> over <code>DENSE_RANK</code>?","a":"<p>When the gap carries meaning, as in sports or competitions where two second places mean nobody is third. Use <code>DENSE_RANK</code> when you want consecutive rank numbers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0158","card":"q-sql-048-dense-rank","sec":"sql-s048","q":"How does <code>DENSE_RANK</code> differ from <code>RANK</code> for salaries 100, 90, 90, 80?","a":"<p><code>DENSE_RANK</code> gives 1, 2, 2, 3, with no gap after the tie. <code>RANK</code> gives 1, 2, 2, 4.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0159","card":"q-sql-048-dense-rank","sec":"sql-s048","q":"How do you find the nth highest salary and why do you use <code>DENSE_RANK</code> for it?","a":"<p>Rank salaries in a CTE with <code>DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY Salary DESC)</code> and select <code>WHERE rnk = n</code>. <code>DENSE_RANK</code> has no gaps, so the 2nd highest distinct salary is rank 2 even when the top salary is shared. <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> would return a duplicate of the top value, and <code>RANK</code> could skip rank 2.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0160","card":"q-sql-048-dense-rank","sec":"sql-s048","q":"For the same data, which of the three functions, <code>ROW_NUMBER</code>, <code>RANK</code> and <code>DENSE_RANK</code>, would you choose to deduplicate, to rank with ties and gaps, and to rank with ties and no gaps?","a":"<p>Deduplicate or choose exactly one row per group: <code>ROW_NUMBER</code>. Competition ranking with gaps: <code>RANK</code>. Consecutive ranks with ties, such as nth distinct value: <code>DENSE_RANK</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0161","card":"q-sql-049-ntile","sec":"sql-s049","q":"What does <code>NTILE(4)</code> do?","a":"<p>It splits the ordered rows into 4 groups of nearly equal size and returns the group number (1 to 4) for each row. Use it for quartiles, deciles and percentile buckets, for example to find the top 25% of customers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0162","card":"q-sql-049-ntile","sec":"sql-s049","q":"How does <code>NTILE</code> handle a row count that does not divide evenly?","a":"<p>The groups differ by at most one row, and the larger groups come first. For 10 rows and <code>NTILE(3)</code>, the groups have 4, 3 and 3 rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0163","card":"q-sql-049-ntile","sec":"sql-s049","q":"How is <code>NTILE</code> different from <code>RANK</code> for splitting data into top and bottom halves?","a":"<p><code>NTILE(2)</code> always makes two equal-size groups, even if tied values land in different groups. <code>RANK</code> gives tied rows the same rank and does not control the group sizes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0164","card":"q-sql-050-invoices-for-customers-with-a-balance-of","sec":"sql-s050","q":"Write a query that lists invoice number, customer name and amount for customers whose balance is at least 1000, given <code>customer(cust_num, cust_lname, cust_fname, cust_balance)</code> and <code>invoice(invoice_num, cust_num, inv_date, inv_amount)</code>.","a":"<p>Join on the shared key and filter on the customer column: <code>SELECT i.invoice_num, c.cust_lname, c.cust_fname, i.inv_amount FROM customer c JOIN invoice i ON c.cust_num = i.cust_num WHERE c.cust_balance &gt;= 1000</code>. Use <code>&gt;=</code> because \"or more\" includes 1000. Prefix shared column names such as <code>cust_num</code> with an alias, otherwise SQL Server reports an ambiguous column.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0165","card":"q-sql-051-fill-null-values-with-isnull","sec":"sql-s051","q":"What does <code>ISNULL(expr, value)</code> do in SQL Server, and how does it differ from <code>COALESCE</code>?","a":"<p>It returns <code>value</code> if <code>expr</code> is NULL, otherwise <code>expr</code>. <code>ISNULL</code> is T-SQL with exactly two arguments, and its result type is the type of the first argument (so a longer replacement string can be truncated). <code>COALESCE</code> is ANSI standard, accepts many arguments, returns the first non-NULL, and picks the type by data type precedence. Prefer <code>COALESCE</code> for portability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0166","card":"q-sql-051-fill-null-values-with-isnull","sec":"sql-s051","q":"How can you fill a NULL value in one row from another row of the same table?","a":"<p>Self join the table on the shared key but with a different row id, and take the value with <code>ISNULL(a.col, b.col)</code>. For example, rows with the same property key but a different row id are assumed to have the same address, so a row with a missing address can copy it from its twin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0167","card":"q-sql-051-fill-null-values-with-isnull","sec":"sql-s051","q":"How do you update a table using values from a self join in SQL Server?","a":"<p>Use <code>UPDATE a SET col = ISNULL(a.col, b.col) FROM t a JOIN t b ON a.key = b.key AND a.id &lt;&gt; b.id WHERE a.col IS NULL</code>. The alias after <code>UPDATE</code> names the table being changed. <code>UPDATE ... FROM</code> is T-SQL syntax; PostgreSQL uses <code>UPDATE t SET ... FROM t2 WHERE ...</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0168","card":"q-sql-051-fill-null-values-with-isnull","sec":"sql-s051","q":"When self joining a table to fill gaps from other rows with the same key (<code>ON a.key = b.key AND a.id &lt;&gt; b.id</code>), why is <code>a.id &lt;&gt; b.id</code> needed?","a":"<p>Without it each row also matches itself, which adds no new information. The condition pairs a row only with a different row that shares the key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0169","card":"q-sql-052-split-a-column-by-delimiter","sec":"sql-s052","q":"How do you split <code>'12 Main St, Springfield'</code> into address and city with <code>SUBSTRING</code> and <code>CHARINDEX</code> in SQL Server?","a":"<p>Address: <code>SUBSTRING(col, 1, CHARINDEX(',', col) - 1)</code>. City: <code>SUBSTRING(col, CHARINDEX(',', col) + 1, LEN(col))</code>. <code>CHARINDEX</code> returns the position of the comma, so <code>-1</code> stops before it and <code>+1</code> starts after it. The city keeps a leading space unless you also <code>TRIM</code> it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0170","card":"q-sql-052-split-a-column-by-delimiter","sec":"sql-s052","q":"What happens when the delimiter is not in the string?","a":"<p><code>CHARINDEX</code> returns 0, so the length becomes -1 and <code>SUBSTRING</code> raises an error. Guard with <code>CASE WHEN CHARINDEX(',', col) &gt; 0 ...</code> or use <code>NULLIF</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0171","card":"q-sql-052-split-a-column-by-delimiter","sec":"sql-s052","q":"What are the steps to store the split parts permanently in SQL Server?","a":"<p>Add new columns with <code>ALTER TABLE ... ADD</code>, then fill them with <code>UPDATE ... SET newcol = &lt;expression&gt;</code>. The columns must exist first. Check the expression with <code>SELECT</code> before updating.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0172","card":"q-sql-052-split-a-column-by-delimiter","sec":"sql-s052","q":"What is <code>STRING_SPLIT</code> and how does it differ from the <code>SUBSTRING</code> approach?","a":"<p><code>STRING_SPLIT(str, sep)</code> (SQL Server 2016+) returns one row per piece (a table), while <code>SUBSTRING</code>/<code>CHARINDEX</code> extracts pieces into columns. Row order is not guaranteed unless you use the <code>enable_ordinal</code> argument added in SQL Server 2022. Use it for lists, <code>SUBSTRING</code> for fixed positions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0173","card":"q-sql-052-split-a-column-by-delimiter","sec":"sql-s052","q":"Why is storing several values in one column such as an address with a comma a design smell, and what is the fix?","a":"<p>It breaks first normal form: values are not atomic, so filtering and indexing by city is awkward. Store the parts in separate columns (or related tables) at load time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0174","card":"q-sql-053-split-a-column-with-parsename","sec":"sql-s053","q":"How does <code>PARSENAME</code> work and why is it used for splitting?","a":"<p><code>PARSENAME(string, n)</code> splits a string on <code>.</code> and returns the nth piece counted from the right (1 is the last piece). It is shorter than <code>SUBSTRING</code> and <code>CHARINDEX</code> for strings with a few parts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0175","card":"q-sql-053-split-a-column-with-parsename","sec":"sql-s053","q":"Why is <code>REPLACE</code> used together with <code>PARSENAME</code> to split a comma-separated address?","a":"<p><code>PARSENAME</code> only understands the dot as separator, so <code>REPLACE(col, ',', '.')</code> converts the commas first. For <code>'a, b, c'</code> piece 3 is <code>a</code>, piece 2 is <code>b</code> and piece 1 is <code>c</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0176","card":"q-sql-053-split-a-column-with-parsename","sec":"sql-s053","q":"What are the limits of <code>PARSENAME</code>?","a":"<p>It handles at most four parts (it is meant for four-part object names) and returns NULL when there are more. It treats dots in the data as separators, so values like <code>St. John</code> break it. For arbitrary delimiters or lengths use <code>STRING_SPLIT</code> or <code>SUBSTRING</code>/<code>CHARINDEX</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0177","card":"q-sql-054-remove-duplicate-records","sec":"sql-s054","q":"How do you find and delete duplicate rows while keeping one copy, in SQL Server?","a":"<p>Number rows within each group of identical values with <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY &lt;all duplicate-defining columns&gt; ORDER BY &lt;id&gt;)</code> in a CTE, then delete rows with <code>row_num &gt; 1</code>. <code>DELETE FROM cte WHERE row_num &gt; 1</code> removes the rows from the underlying table. Always run it as a <code>SELECT</code> first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0178","card":"q-sql-054-remove-duplicate-records","sec":"sql-s054","q":"In the <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> duplicate approach, what should go in <code>PARTITION BY</code> and what in <code>ORDER BY</code>?","a":"<p><code>PARTITION BY</code> lists the columns that define \"the same record\", and it must not include the unique id (otherwise nothing looks duplicated). <code>ORDER BY</code> decides which copy gets number 1 and survives, for example the lowest id or the newest date.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0179","card":"q-sql-054-remove-duplicate-records","sec":"sql-s054","q":"What alternatives exist for removing duplicates, and when are they better?","a":"<p><code>SELECT DISTINCT ... INTO new_table</code> then swap the tables (simple, but copies the data and loses constraints). <code>GROUP BY</code> with <code>MIN(id)</code> to select the ids to keep, then delete the others. The window approach is the most flexible because you choose which copy to keep.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"sql-f0180","card":"q-sql-054-remove-duplicate-records","sec":"sql-s054","q":"How do you prevent duplicates from coming back?","a":"<p>Add a <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint or unique index on the natural key columns, and handle conflicts on load (for example <code>MERGE</code> or <code>INSERT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS</code>).</p>","hand":true}];
